Rize.io: Exhaustive Technical and Architectural Reverse-Engineering ReportThe following comprehensive architectural breakdown and visual blueprint reverse-engineers the Rize.io productivity and time-tracking application. By dissecting the application across its core operational layers, this analysis translates passive metadata collection, algorithmic processing, generative AI categorization, and user interface paradigms into a highly structured technical specification. The architectural philosophy underlying the platform pivots away from manual timer discipline and invasive surveillance, favoring instead a privacy-first, metadata-driven approach that leverages advanced Large Language Models to establish operational context.Core Tracking EngineThe tracking engine serves as the foundational data ingestion layer, operating silently as a background daemon to capture operating system-level application context while maintaining a strict, cryptography-grade privacy envelope. This architectural decision is pivotal for enterprise adoption, as it drastically reduces the friction typically associated with employee monitoring software.Active Window Metadata Observer (MVP Core)Functional Mechanics: The observer daemon relies on native operating system APIs (such as NSWorkspace and Accessibility/Automation permissions via AXUIElement on macOS) to continuously monitor the frontmost active window. It executes a low-overhead background polling loop, capturing the bundleIdentifier, raw window title string, and precise UNIX timestamp boundaries. To prevent database fragmentation and API spam, the engine utilizes a 5-second debounce buffer, effectively filtering out micro-switches such as quickly glancing at a notification or a chat window. Crucially, the system strictly enforces a metadata-only boundary; it explicitly bypasses CGEventTap for keylogging and ignores frame buffer APIs, ensuring no screenshots or keystrokes are ever captured.Visual Mapping & UI Elements: The observer is headless by design but projects its real-time status through a 16x16 pixel interactive power icon anchored in the bottom-left corner of the primary application sidebar. The icon employs semantic state colors to communicate system status at a glance: vibrant green (#34C759) for active tracking, subdued gray (#8E8E93) for manually paused states, and an amber warning indicator if the host OS revokes necessary permissions.Data Displayed: The raw ingested data is surfaced in the "Categories > Activity Log" table. The display strictly limits output to four primary columns: the Application Name (string), Window Title (string), URL (string, if applicable), and total duration (a calculated integer in seconds derived from the start and end timestamps).User Flow & Micro-interactions: The user interaction is largely confined to the global pause/resume toggle. Hovering over the power icon triggers a custom tooltip with a 150ms delay, displaying the current background tracking status. A primary click toggles the state, immediately halting the OS polling loop and triggering a transient system-level native notification confirming the state change. If accessibility permissions are revoked by the OS, clicking the icon triggers a modal overlay providing a deep link directly to System Settings > Privacy & Security to resolve the blocker.Cross-Browser URL Interceptor (MVP Core)Functional Mechanics: Web browser architecture requires varying interception strategies depending on the host operating system. For Chromium-based and Firefox browsers on Windows, URL extraction necessitates a companion browser extension communicating via native messaging hosts. Conversely, on macOS, the application leverages OS-level AppleScript and Automation permissions to query the active tab's URL directly from the browser's Document Object Model (DOM) without requiring an extension. The interceptor contains strict fallback logic: if a browser is running in Incognito or Private mode, or if the necessary permissions are absent, the engine safely degrades the activity classification to a generic "Browsing" string, ensuring private paths are never logged.Visual Mapping & UI Elements: URL tracking configurations are mapped within a dedicated settings card located at Settings > Privacy. The interface utilizes standard native toggle switches for global URL tracking and features a specific, indented granular toggle dedicated to "Domain-Only Tracking".Data Displayed: The Activity Log and the Suggestion Context Panel present the parsed URL strings. If the domain-only tracking setting is enabled, the UI applies a regex truncation filter, reducing a highly specific path like https://docs.google.com/document/d/123/edit to display purely as docs.google.com.User Flow & Micro-interactions: When a user first launches a supported browser on macOS, they are presented with a native OS permission prompt requesting Automation access. Within the Rize settings UI, toggling the "Domain-Only" switch immediately updates the local configuration store, applying the truncation logic pre-flight so that subsequent database writes are anonymized before local storage insertion.Tracking VectormacOS ImplementationWindows ImplementationData CapturedPrivacy ConstraintDesktop ApplicationsNSWorkspace / AccessibilityWin32 API / UIAutomationApp Name, Window TitleNo window contents, no keystrokes.Web Browsers (Standard)AppleScript / OS AutomationChrome/Firefox ExtensionFull URL stringDomain-only truncation option available.Web Browsers (Incognito)System bypassExtension disablement"Browsing" string onlyComplete URL path suppression.Automated Tracking Scheduler (Advanced)Functional Mechanics: The scheduler functions as a chronological, cron-style service that continuously compares the local system time against a user-defined work matrix. It automatically enables and disables the Active Window Metadata Observer based on localized day-of-week and time parameters. This ensures the tracking daemon sleeps entirely during off-hours, preserving laptop battery life and enforcing strict employee privacy during personal time.Visual Mapping & UI Elements: Housed in Settings > Activity > Automatic Tracking Schedule. The UI presents a seven-day horizontal array of pill-shaped day toggles, paired with start and end time picker components (e.g., 09:00 AM to 05:00 PM). Unselected days are visually grayed out, reducing visual weight and indicating inactivity.Data Displayed: Time boundaries are displayed as standard 12-hour or 24-hour localized strings depending on the OS locale settings. Days of the week are represented by their first initial (M, T, W, T, F, S, S).User Flow & Micro-interactions: The user clicks a day pill to activate or deactivate scheduling for that specific day. Clicking the time input opens a native dropdown menu for precise selection. If a user manually overrides the schedule (for example, clicking the global pause button during scheduled active hours), a visual flag is set in the state manager, and the scheduler will not aggressively re-engage until the next scheduled chronological boundary is crossed.Scheduled Data Redaction Service (Advanced)Functional Mechanics: A destructive garbage-collection pipeline designed explicitly for enterprise compliance, GDPR adherence, and strict privacy mandates. When enabled, a background job routinely queries the local and remote SQL databases for raw activity logs (specific window titles and URLs) older than the user-defined threshold and executes a permanent DELETE operation. The system mathematically abstracts the raw data into higher-level productivity metrics and generic category aggregates before redaction. This ensures that historical charts and profitability reports remain structurally intact while the underlying forensic data is irreversibly destroyed.Visual Mapping & UI Elements: Located in Settings > Privacy. The interface features a prominent, red-tinted destructive action button labeled "Redact Tracked Data" for immediate manual overrides, situated alongside a dropdown selector for the automated redaction schedule.Data Displayed: The dropdown presents predefined retention intervals (e.g., "Keep data for 7 days," "30 days," "90 days"). A secondary helper text block explicitly outlines the architectural reality: metadata will be deleted while category aggregates are preserved, setting clear user expectations.User Flow & Micro-interactions: Selecting a scheduled interval saves the preference silently with a subtle green checkmark micro-animation. Clicking the manual "Redact Tracked Data" button invokes a high-friction confirmation modal with a dark scrim overlay. The user must explicitly click a secondary confirmation button, confirming the irreversible destruction of data, before the API fires the redaction payload to the server.Main Daily Timeline & Suggestion InboxThe visual core of the application prioritizes temporal spatial awareness. It utilizes a calendar-like spatial mapping to represent time expenditure, paired with a sophisticated contextual review panel to bridge raw tracking data with human validation.Vertical Chronological Timeline (MVP Core)Functional Mechanics: A highly optimized render loop calculates the absolute start and end times of discrete activity blocks and maps them to Y-axis pixel coordinates based on a configurable timescale. The timeline supports real-time rendering; as the background tracker runs, a "live tracking block" expands pixel-by-pixel downwards on the timeline. If the user transitions to a new context, the live block is finalized, and a new block begins.Visual Mapping & UI Elements: Dominating the center of the main dashboard, the UI is a vertical daily calendar grid highly reminiscent of Apple Calendar's day view. Hours are marked on the left Y-axis. Time blocks are rendered as solid rectangular bars. Every block features a distinct 4px vertical colored stripe on its extreme left edge denoting its specific Team or Workspace affiliation, which is critical for multi-org users. The primary columns (Activity, Time Entries, Sessions, Calendar) can be dynamically reordered via drag-and-drop or hidden entirely.Data Displayed: Each rectangular block displays the entry title, total duration (e.g., "1h 15m"), and horizontal pill tags for Client, Project, and Task.User Flow & Micro-interactions: Users can drag and drop the top or bottom edges of a block; the cursor changes to an ns-resize indicator, and dragging recalculates the temporal boundaries of the entry in real-time, instantly updating the underlying database record. Hovering over a live activity block reveals a dynamic tooltip of real-time metadata. Users can drag column headers horizontally to reorder the view, with the layout state persisting via client caching so the timeline opens exactly as they left it.Suggestion Review Panel (Drawer) (MVP Core)Functional Mechanics: This component acts as a dynamic context aggregator. When a pending time entry is selected, it fetches relational data from the local data store, compiling a unified view of all raw tracking events, connected calendar events, and AI tags associated with that specific timestamp boundary. This drawer entirely replaces legacy batch-inbox systems by providing immediate side-by-side context.Visual Mapping & UI Elements: Triggered by interaction, this UI component is a full-height, ~400px wide sliding drawer that pushes in from the right edge of the application viewport, overlapping the timeline slightly with a subtle drop shadow. It is segmented into vertical sections: a header with quick actions, an editable Title/Description text block, an AI Suggestions card, and a scrollable Activity Context log with nested tabs for titles, apps, event logs, and tags.Data Displayed: Displays the generated Title, Description, Confidence Scores, a chronological list of raw app/URL metadata captured during the block, and overlapping calendar event data.User Flow & Micro-interactions: Clicking a pending time block on the timeline slides the drawer open using a fluid ease-in-out cubic bezier transition. The user can click directly into the Title field to edit text. Hovering over AI tags reveals an 'x' icon to dismiss incorrect suggestions. Users can natively create a new ClickUp or Linear task directly from an inline dropdown within the panel without opening a new browser tab. Clicking the primary "Approve" button commits the entry and automatically shifts focus to the next chronological pending block, facilitating a rapid, frictionless triage workflow.Inline Time Block Segmentation (Split/Merge) (Advanced)Functional Mechanics: Advanced mathematical array manipulation tools for temporal management. The "Merge" function calculates the minimum start time and maximum end time of two or more adjacent blocks, creating a unified time block and concatenating their metadata arrays. The "Split" function halves a block chronologically or allows proportional division based on user input, distributing the raw tracking events into the two newly created child blocks based on their precise timestamps.Visual Mapping & UI Elements: Available as quick-action icon buttons (representing branching or converging arrows) situated in the header of the Suggestion Review Panel, or via a contextual right-click menu natively invoked on the timeline blocks.Data Displayed: When initiating a split, a modal overlay presents a timeline scrubber or numerical inputs to define the exact split point (e.g., a 50/50 split, or explicitly typing 2:15 PM).User Flow & Micro-interactions: Selecting two adjacent blocks on the timeline and pressing the global hotkey CMD/CTRL + SHIFT + U instantly executes a merge operation, animating the two rectangular UI nodes morphing into a single continuous block. Clicking "Split" opens a popover; adjusting the slider visually splits the block in real-time, and clicking confirm finalizes the database array mutation.Voice Dictation & Manual Entry Modals (MVP Core)Functional Mechanics: While the platform optimizes for passive tracking, manual entry remains a necessary fallback. Centralized CRUD (Create, Read, Update, Delete) modals enforce relational integrity (e.g., ensuring a Task belongs to a Project) and map entries to the workspace's GraphQL schema. An advanced voice dictation layer actively parses spoken natural language, utilizing entity recognition to detect mentioned clients, and cross-references the timeline to proactively flag overlapping entry conflicts prior to database insertion.Visual Mapping & UI Elements: Clean, floating overlay modals centered on the screen with a semi-transparent black background scrim (rgba(0,0,0,0.5)). They utilize strict 8px grid spacing, standard label-input pairings, and a prominent floating action button (+) located on the Home tab for manual invocation.Data Displayed: The forms feature input fields for Name, Keywords, Color (hex selector), Status, Client mapping dropdowns, Time Budgets, Due Dates, and a prominent toggle switch for marking the entity as "Billable".User Flow & Micro-interactions: Triggered via the + button, the user navigates the modal via standard keyboard Tab indexing. Typing keywords and hitting Enter immediately formats the string into a colored tag pill. Clicking the "Billable" toggle activates a fluid sliding switch animation. Hitting CMD+Enter saves the entity and dismisses the modal with a subtle scale-down animation. If voice dictation detects a conflict, a red inline warning string appears, requiring user resolution before the save event can proceed.Rules and AI Categorization EngineThis layer acts as the application's cerebral cortex. It transitions raw, disparate metadata into normalized, contextually aware human work blocks using a hybrid approach of deterministic heuristics and stochastic Large Language Model (LLM) embeddings.GPT-5 Semantic Suggestion Generator (Advanced)Functional Mechanics: When raw tracking events accumulate, the engine executes a grouping algorithm to cluster continuous, related activities into "logical work blocks". These clusters are serialized into a payload encompassing app names, window titles, URLs, and time boundaries, which is securely transmitted to a GPT-5 powered inference server. The AI utilizes semantic embeddings and vector similarity to map the activity cluster against the user's predefined Clients, Projects, and Tasks. It bypasses rigid keyword matching, allowing it to infer that a file named "Q3_Financials.xlsx" belongs to the "Accounting" project, generating natural language titles like "Code review on authentication module".Visual Mapping & UI Elements: The output is routed directly to the Suggestion Review Panel and the main timeline. Suggestions appear as interactive cards featuring an AI sparkle icon (✨) to denote their machine origin. The cards use skeleton loading animations while awaiting the server-side LLM response to mask latency.Data Displayed: The UI renders the AI-generated natural language title, a descriptive summary string, and tag pills adorned with a color-coded Confidence Score percentage (e.g., 92% in green, 65% in yellow). Additionally, a plain-English explanation string is appended to the UI (e.g., "Suggested based on matching project keywords"), demystifying the black-box nature of the AI.User Flow & Micro-interactions: The user clicks on a suggested time block. Hovering over the confidence score percentage triggers a tooltip displaying the exact signal vectors the AI used (calendar events, keywords, or historical patterns) to reach its conclusion. The user reviews the AI-generated card and clicks "Approve," transitioning its visual state from a pending dashed border to a solid confirmed block.Hierarchical Tracking Rule Engine (MVP Core)Functional Mechanics: A deterministic fallback and pre-processor engine that evaluates metadata before AI inference. It utilizes strict string matching (exact, substring, or regex) to map an application, website, or specific window title to a predefined Category or exclusion list. The rules are evaluated sequentially in a strict hierarchy: specific URL matches (e.g., docs.google.com/doc/1) override domain rules (docs.google.com), which in turn override application-level rules (Google Chrome). Unmatched items default to a "Miscellaneous" state for exactly 2 minutes before the AI is invoked to categorize it, or defaults to "Uncategorized" after 10 minutes if AI categorization is globally disabled.Visual Mapping & UI Elements: Located in Settings > Tracking Rules. The interface is a dense, searchable, paginated data table. Each row displays the source (App/Website), the matching logic string, and a dropdown for the assigned Category. An "Add New Rule" button sits prominently at the top right.Data Displayed: The table displays the App Icon, Source String, Category Pill (color-coded), and an action menu (ellipsis) for editing, deleting, or temporarily disabling the rule.User Flow & Micro-interactions: Clicking "New Rule" triggers a modal overlay. The user selects the match type ("Categorize app or website"), inputs the target string, optionally inputs a substring for the window title, and selects the target category from a searchable dropdown menu. Clicking "Save" instantly writes the rule to the local SQLite database and automatically recalibrates the current day's active data against the new heuristic, updating the timeline without requiring a manual refresh.Dynamic Tagging Depth Controller (Advanced)Functional Mechanics: An algorithmic governor that dictates the aggression level of the AI auto-tagging system. It evaluates the raw probability output of the LLM against user-defined organizational thresholds. A "High Depth" setting forces the system to always attempt to append granular Task-level tags even if the AI's confidence is statistically moderate, prioritizing automation over perfect accuracy. A "Low Depth" setting restricts the AI to assigning broad Client or Project tags unless the confidence vector exceeds a very high threshold (e.g., >90%), favoring a conservative, highly accurate approach.Visual Mapping & UI Elements: Nested inside Settings > Time Entries under the "Tagging Policy" section. The UI is presented as a segmented control or a stepped slider labeled "Tagging Depth", with distinct stops for Conservative, Balanced, and Aggressive.Data Displayed: Explanatory microcopy beneath the slider updates dynamically as the user drags the control, explicitly explaining the trade-off between manual review reduction and the risk of misclassification.User Flow & Micro-interactions: The user clicks and drags the slider thumb. A localized haptic feedback event triggers at each stop (via NSHapticFeedbackManager on macOS). The selection instantly updates the JSON inference prompt structure sent to the backend for all subsequent clustering operations.Generative Custom Instructions Pipeline (Advanced)Functional Mechanics: A prompt-injection pipeline that prepends user-defined textual rules directly into the system prompt of the GPT-5 categorization model. This allows organizations to strictly dictate the tone, casing, verb tense, and structural formatting of AI-generated Activity Summaries without undergoing the expense and complexity of fine-tuning the base model.Visual Mapping & UI Elements: Located within Settings > Time Entries. Displayed as an expanding accordion section titled "Custom Instructions," containing multiple multi-line text area inputs corresponding to different generation targets (e.g., Activity Summaries, Task Suggestions, Project Suggestions).Data Displayed: Free-form text fields containing the user's custom prompt strings.User Flow & Micro-interactions: The user clicks into a text area and types behavioral constraints (e.g., "Always format titles as '[Action] - [Component]' and use past tense"). The field utilizes a standard active-state border highlight (e.g., a 2px #0A84FF focus ring). Changes are saved via a debounced auto-save function, indicated by a subtle "Saved" label fading in and out next to the section header, preventing data loss during configuration.Zero-Touch Auto-Approval Engine (Advanced)Functional Mechanics: An automation bypass mechanism designed for power users that skips the human review loop entirely. When enabled, any generated time entry suggestion that meets the user's configured confidence threshold is automatically serialized, committed to the primary database, and appended to the final timeline without ever entering the pending queue.Visual Mapping & UI Elements: A master toggle switch in Settings > Time Entries labeled "Automatically Approve Suggestions." When toggled on, it reveals a subordinate dropdown menu labeled "Default Workspace".Data Displayed: The dropdown lists all available workspaces the user belongs to, ensuring that the automated entries are routed to the correct billing context and do not cross-pollinate organizational data.User Flow & Micro-interactions: Flipping the toggle transitions its background to a highly visible active state (green). The user selects the default workspace. From that moment, the user's timeline populates autonomously. The user can still interact with auto-approved blocks on the timeline to retroactively edit or split them, following standard CRUD operations.Focus and Wellness ToolsGoing beyond passive data tracking, this layer actively intervenes in the user's workflow. By utilizing behavioral psychology paradigms—such as urge surfing and contextual blocking—the application enforces deep work states and monitors cognitive fatigue.Algorithmic Focus Session Calculator (MVP Core)Functional Mechanics: An aggressive, threshold-based evaluation engine running continuously in the background. The algorithm divides time into rolling 15-minute windows. It calculates the total duration of activity within that window that matches categories explicitly flagged as "Focus Categories" (e.g., IDEs, design tools). If the calculated sum exceeds a 75% threshold (11.25 minutes), the engine retroactively flags the entire 15-minute block as a valid "Focus Session," smoothing over minor unavoidable distractions.Visual Mapping & UI Elements: Focus sessions are mapped onto the primary timeline and a dedicated "Sessions" tab. They are visually distinguished from standard time entries by unique color coding (often a deep purple or blue) and a distinctive session badge.Data Displayed: The interface outputs the calculated total Focus Time, which mathematically may not explicitly match the raw time spent in Focus Categories due to the 75% threshold padding mechanism attributing the full 15 minutes.User Flow & Micro-interactions: The session is generated entirely autonomously based on the user's organic behavior. However, a user can manually trigger a Focus Session by clicking a "Start Session" button in the desktop widget, which bypasses the 15-minute threshold requirement, initiates a live countdown timer, and locks the tracking state.Break & Audio Wellness Module (Advanced)Functional Mechanics: A restorative counter-measure to continuous deep work. The system tracks idle time to automatically classify breaks, but also provides a structured manual break timer. To support the session, the application integrates an audio playback engine that streams built-in lo-fi music during focus sessions, and transitions to streaming guided meditations during configured break periods. The system supports default break duration configurations (typically 10, 20, or up to 120 minutes) and enforces a maximum break duration to prevent sessions from sitting paused indefinitely.Visual Mapping & UI Elements: Configured in Settings > Breaks and accessible via the floating desktop widget. The UI presents dropdowns for default durations and playback toggles.Data Displayed: A live countdown timer indicating the time remaining in the break, alongside the title of the currently playing audio track.User Flow & Micro-interactions: When a user initiates a break via the widget, a modal appears allowing them to select the break length. Once started, the music fades out, and the guided meditation audio track fades in. When the timer reaches zero, a gentle chime plays, signaling the user to return to work, and the audio state resets.Distraction Blocker Overlay (Advanced)Functional Mechanics: An active intervention service. While a Focus Session is active, the system's tracking loop cross-references the active window against categories marked as "Distracting" (e.g., Social Media, Messaging). If the continuous duration in a distracting application exceeds a user-defined threshold (e.g., 30 seconds), the engine triggers an OS-level window override to interrupt the user's visual context.Visual Mapping & UI Elements: Configurable in Settings > Focus > Distraction Blocker. When triggered, it renders either as a native system notification (sliding in from the top right on macOS) or, more aggressively, as a modal pop-up window centered on the screen, completely occluding the distracting content beneath it.Data Displayed: The overlay displays a gentle, customizable nudge text (e.g., "You are currently in a Focus Session. Are you sure you want to browse Twitter?") and renders options to dismiss the blocker or close the distracting application.User Flow & Micro-interactions: Once the temporal threshold is breached, the blocker instantly appears, stealing window focus. The user must actively click a dismissal button to regain access to the underlying application, intentionally breaking the autopilot loop of distraction.Urge Surfing Delay Mechanism (Advanced)Functional Mechanics: A behavioral psychology tool tied directly to the Distraction Blocker. When enabled, it disables the dismissal function of the distraction modal for a hard-coded 10-second temporal delay. This forces the user to experience the "urge" to be distracted without immediately gratifying it, often leading to the user abandoning the distraction entirely.Visual Mapping & UI Elements: Located within the Distraction Blocker settings. When triggered on the active pop-up window, the primary "Dismiss" button is rendered in a visually disabled state (opacity 0.5, grayed out, with no hover effect).Data Displayed: A live, 10-second countdown timer is displayed prominently within the disabled Dismiss button.User Flow & Micro-interactions: The user encounters the distraction modal. They attempt to click "Dismiss," but the cursor displays a not-allowed symbol. The user must wait as the 10-second timer decrements to zero. Only upon reaching zero does the button transition to an active, clickable state, allowing the user to bypass the blocker.Focus Quality Score Engine (Advanced)Functional Mechanics: A proprietary multivariate assessment algorithm that runs post-session. It aggregates over 20 distinct behavioral attributes during a completed Focus Session—including the frequency of OS-level context switches (Alt-Tabbing), the duration of micro-distractions, and the pure ratio of time spent in Focus versus Non-Focus categories.Visual Mapping & UI Elements: The score is visualized prominently within the "Trends" tab of the analytics sidebar. It is typically rendered as a large, dynamic radial gauge or a bold numerical score (0-100) colored green (excellent), yellow (fair), or red (poor).Data Displayed: Alongside the master score, the UI lists the exact negative vectors that impacted the score, labeled as "Top Interrupters."Focus Score AttributeMeasurement VectorImpact on ScoreContext SwitchingFrequency of active window changes per minuteHigh NegativeApp DistractionsTime spent in Non-Focus categories during sessionSevere NegativeSession LengthTotal continuous duration of the Focus blockPositiveInterruption CountNumber of times the Distraction Blocker was triggeredModerate NegativeUser Flow & Micro-interactions: The user navigates to the Trends dashboard. Hovering over specific data points on the line graph reveals historical Focus Quality Scores. Clicking a specific "Top Interrupter" dynamically filters the view to show exactly when those interruptions occurred on the chronological timeline, allowing the user to identify their weakest points of the day.Always-On Floating Desktop Widget (Advanced)Functional Mechanics: A lightweight, decoupled UI layer running as a separate frameless window process (typically a transparent Electron BrowserWindow). It remains pinned to the highest Z-index of the desktop (Always on Top) to provide ambient awareness without requiring the main application to be in focus.Visual Mapping & UI Elements: A compact, pill-shaped overlay positioned by default in the top right corner of the OS desktop. The widget features a highly minimized UI with circular progress rings and bold typography.Data Displayed: Dynamically switches context based on the engine state. During a break, it displays the time elapsed since the last break. During a session, it displays the live Session Timer. It also features a progress ring indicating the percentage of the day's Work Hours target achieved.User Flow & Micro-interactions: By default, the widget employs a proximity auto-hide feature; hovering the mouse cursor over the widget causes it to collapse or fade out to prevent occluding underlying OS menus. Clicking the widget reveals a quick-action dropdown menu allowing the user to pause tracking, start a session, or open the main dashboard. The user can freely click and drag the widget to reposition it across multiple monitors, with its coordinates saved locally.Analytics and Metrics DashboardThis layer synthesizes thousands of daily micro-events into macro-level insights, enabling individuals and managers to evaluate temporal allocation, capacity limits, and operational bottlenecks over extended timelines.Dual-Mode Work Hours Aggregator (MVP Core)Functional Mechanics: The calculation engine responsible for quantifying total daily output. The system supports two distinct mathematical methodologies via a settings toggle to accommodate different user philosophies. The modern default, "Time Entries Mode," calculates total Work Hours by strictly summing the durations of all pending and approved time blocks on the timeline (excluding gaps and breaks). The legacy "Categories Mode" calculates the sum of time spent in Focus, Meeting, and Break blocks, plus any raw time spent in categories explicitly flagged as "Work".Visual Mapping & UI Elements: Displayed in the "Daily Summary" panel on the right side of the Home tab, and visualized as a horizontal progress bar charting progress toward a user-defined daily target. The bar features team-color segmentation, visually slicing the bar into different colors based on where the time was allocated.Data Displayed: Outputs the absolute number of Work Hours logged (e.g., "6h 45m") and a percentage of the daily target (e.g., "85%").User Flow & Micro-interactions: The user accesses Settings > Time Entries to select the calculation method from a dropdown. In the Daily Summary, hovering over the segmented Work Hours progress bar triggers a tooltip breaking down exactly how many hours were contributed by each distinct workspace/team. To modify a target for a specific day retroactively (or set a unique target for a specific day of the week, like a half-day Friday), the user clicks a cog icon in the summary panel and selects a new hour target from a native dropdown list.Focus Trends & Interrupter Visualizer (Advanced)Functional Mechanics: A data visualization layer that queries historical tracking data across extended temporal ranges (weekly, monthly, yearly). It aggregates the raw data to identify macro-trends in focus capability and highlights systemic workflow interruptions.Visual Mapping & UI Elements: Housed under the "Productivity" section in the left sidebar, specifically the "Trends" tab. The UI is dominated by responsive line charts (for Focus Score over time) and stacked bar charts or pie charts categorizing daily distribution.Data Displayed: Renders comprehensive metrics: total Focus Score trend lines, average session length, total focus time per day, and a ranked list of the "Top Interrupters" (specific applications or websites that most frequently broke continuous focus).User Flow & Micro-interactions: Users interact with the charts by hovering over nodes to view specific daily data tooltips. They can toggle the temporal scope (Week, Month, Year) using segmented controls at the top right of the viewport. Changing the scope triggers a fluid redraw animation of the SVG/Canvas charting elements, smoothly interpolating the data points to their new positions.Team, Manager, and Profitability DashboardTargeted at agency operators, client-services teams, and administrators, this infrastructure transforms raw individual time data into organizational financial optics, blending utilization tracking with complex revenue recognition models. This elevates the software from a personal productivity tool to a critical B2B operational ledger.Tri-Modal Billing & Revenue Recognition Engine (Advanced)Functional Mechanics: A financial calculation pipeline that computes operational profitability by linking tracked time entries to specific client billing models and labor cost rates. The engine runs a daily background job to update statistics based on three supported commercial models. The primary B2B value proposition lies in identifying premature margins—highlighting when a project is bleeding cash before the billing cycle ends.Billing ModelRecognition LogicRevenue Calculation ExampleHourlyDirect multiplication of time and rate.10 hours tracked $\times$ $100/hr = $1,000.RetainerFlat fee divided by days in billing period.$3,000/mo = ~$100/day recognized regardless of hours.HybridRetainer up to cap, then hourly overage.20 hours included, subsequent hours at $150/hr.Visual Mapping & UI Elements: Located within the organizational administrative views and the "Profitability" dashboard. The UI utilizes high-density data tables, sparklines, and KPI (Key Performance Indicator) counter cards.Data Displayed: Surfaces calculated Revenue, total Labor Cost, and absolute Profit Margin at four distinct hierarchy levels: Organization-wide, per-Client, per-Project, and per-Member.User Flow & Micro-interactions: A workspace admin navigates to a Client's settings modal. They select the billing type from a dropdown. If "Hybrid" is selected, the UI dynamically expands to reveal two new number input fields: "Included Retainer Hours" and "Overage Rate." Updating these inputs triggers a recalculation job that instantly refreshes the Profitability dashboard charts.Member Cost Margin Calculator (Advanced)Functional Mechanics: To determine the "Cost" variable in the profitability equation ($Profit = Revenue - Cost$), the system maintains an internal, highly secure ledger of labor costs. Each team member is assigned a specific internal hourly cost rate. When a time entry is approved and assigned to a project, the engine cross-references the duration against the assigned member's cost rate, deducting that sum from the client's recognized revenue to determine true profit margins.Visual Mapping & UI Elements: Managed within the "Members View" admin dashboard. A clean list view of all invited team members, featuring columns for Role, Status, and internal Cost Rate.Data Displayed: The cost rate is displayed as a currency-formatted string (e.g., "$55.00/hr"). Strict field-level authentication is applied at the API level, ensuring non-admin members cannot view the cost rates or billing margins of the organization, rendering those columns completely hidden on standard accounts.User Flow & Micro-interactions: An admin clicks a member's row, opening an edit popover. They input the numerical cost rate. Upon saving, the system applies this cost forward, and admins can immediately view the individual member's profitability contribution within the macro Profitability dashboard.Real-Time Team Visibility Dashboard (Advanced)Functional Mechanics: A synchronization service utilizing WebSockets or rapid polling to aggregate the real-time active state of all users within a shared workspace. It provides managers with a bird's-eye view of capacity and current focus states. By mapping state over screenshots, the architecture achieves capacity planning and utilization monitoring without crossing the line into invasive surveillance software.Visual Mapping & UI Elements: A dedicated dashboard view featuring a responsive grid or list of team member cards. Each card acts as a live status indicator.Data Displayed: Displays the user's avatar, their current active project/client tag, and a live tracking block indicating if they are currently in a Focus Session, Meeting, or Break.User Flow & Micro-interactions: Managers can filter the grid by specific clients or teams using a top-level search bar. The cards update autonomously as members transition between contexts, providing a living map of workload allocation across the agency.Granular Organizational Permissions Manager (Advanced)Functional Mechanics: A workspace configuration layer allowing root administrators to dictate the taxonomy and features available to the organization. This prevents data fragmentation by ensuring all members use a unified data model (e.g., preventing rogue members from creating un-billable categories that disrupt profitability reports).Visual Mapping & UI Elements: Found in the main Workspace Settings dashboard. Represented as a list of master toggle switches corresponding to organizational entities.Data Displayed: Master toggles explicitly labeled "Tasks," "Projects," "Clients," "Categories," and "Billable Hours".User Flow & Micro-interactions: If an admin toggles "Tasks" to the Off position, a global state update is pushed to all clients in the workspace via the API. Instantly, the "Tasks" tag pill disappears from all timeline blocks, Suggestion Review Panels, and creation modals for every member, streamlining the UI to strictly track Clients and Projects.Settings and Integrations EcosystemRize operates not as an isolated silo, but as a central node in the modern productivity stack. This layer dictates how the platform ingests context from external tools, communicates with AI agents, and exports structured data to automated workflows.Bidirectional External Task Sync (Advanced)Functional Mechanics: An integration service relying on OAuth 2.0 authorization to external task trackers (ClickUp, Linear, Asana). Once connected, Rize establishes a two-way sync loop. It ingests external issue titles, IDs, and descriptions into its local matching pool, treating them identically to native tasks for AI context embedding. When a user tracks time against an external task, Rize utilizes the external platform's API to push the logged duration back to the source ticket. A specific setting handles mapping issues assigned to non-Rize users to ensure global project tracking remains accurate across an entire agency. Jira, Notion, Monday, and Todoist are supported via Zapier webhooks.Visual Mapping & UI Elements: Located in Settings > Integrations. Each service features a connection card displaying connection status, a branded logo, and a "Configure" button.Data Displayed: Once synced, external tasks appear in the Suggestion Review Panel and dropdowns, distinguished by the native icon of the external tool (e.g., the Linear logo next to the task string "RST-144: Fix Auth Bug").User Flow & Micro-interactions: The user clicks "Connect" on the ClickUp card. They are redirected to a standard OAuth consent screen in their web browser. Upon approval, they are redirected back to Rize via a deep link. Inside the Suggestion panel, typing a keyword instantly auto-completes with matching Linear/Asana tickets. Finalizing the block triggers a background API POST request to the external service, showing a brief loading spinner on the task pill.Calendar Sync & Keyword Parser (MVP Core)Functional Mechanics: The application syncs with Google Calendar and Outlook to overlay scheduled events onto the timeline. Beyond visual mapping, a parser engine scans the event titles and descriptions for specific predefined keywords to automatically instantiate Rize states without user input. The parser also features a privacy toggle to explicitly ignore calendar attendee email addresses to prevent PII leakage.Visual Mapping & UI Elements: Managed in Settings > Calendars. The UI features input fields for mapping specific keywords to Rize states.Data Displayed: By default, the system recognizes #rize-focus, focus, and flow for Focus Sessions; #rize-meeting for Meetings; and #rize-break and break for Break Sessions.User Flow & Micro-interactions: A user creates an event in their Google Calendar titled "Deep Work #rize-focus". When the event's start time arrives, Rize's background parser detects the keyword, suppresses the Distraction Blocker UI, and autonomously initiates a Focus Session tracking loop, creating a seamless bridge between scheduling and execution.OAuth 2.0 Model Context Protocol (MCP) Server (Advanced)Functional Mechanics: Rize exposes an MCP server (https://mcp.rize.io/mcp) that allows local or cloud-based AI assistants (Claude Desktop, ChatGPT, Cursor, Zed) to securely read and write Rize data directly from their native chat interfaces. It utilizes OAuth 2.0 authorization code flow with PKCE, exchanging authorization codes for access tokens valid for 2 hours (with long-lived refresh tokens). The server communicates via Streamable HTTP, dynamically registering clients that follow RFC 7591.MCP Tool NameFunctionalityCapabilitylist_my_time_entriesQueries time entries for a date range, returning status, client, project, and durations.Readget_time_entryFetches a single entry by ID, surfacing billing info and AI confidence data.Readgenerate_time_entriesAnalyzes tracked apps/meetings and clusters them into AI entries via external command.Writelist_my_apps_usedQueries raw app and website usage data for analysis.ReadVisual Mapping & UI Elements: The configuration occurs entirely within the external AI client (e.g., Claude Desktop settings), not inside the Rize UI. Rize's involvement is strictly limited to a web-based consent screen hosted at app.rize.io/oauth/mcp/signin.Data Displayed: The consent screen displays a bulleted list of read/write permissions the AI assistant is requesting over the workspace data.User Flow & Micro-interactions: The user configures the MCP connector in Claude Desktop, setting the transport to Streamable HTTP. Claude opens a browser. The user authenticates via a Google SSO or Magic Link. They review the consent screen and click "Approve." The browser redirects back to the MCP client. The user can now seamlessly type natural language queries into Claude (e.g., "How much time did I track today?"), and the MCP server parses the JSON response directly into the chat interface.Slack Agent (Advanced)Functional Mechanics: A conversational interface bridging Rize data into a team's primary communication hub. The agent utilizes a secure webhook and API connection to allow users to query their time data, team time, project status, and hours directly inside Slack using plain language.Visual Mapping & UI Elements: Operates entirely within the Slack application as an integrated App/Bot.Data Displayed: Returns formatted Slack messages containing project statuses and hour summaries based on the user's natural language query.User Flow & Micro-interactions: The user tags the Rize bot in a Slack channel or DM and asks, "Where is my time going this week?" The bot queries the Rize backend API and replies with a formatted summary of clients and hours.GraphQL API & Webhook Dispatcher (Advanced)Functional Mechanics: For enterprise extensibility, the entire platform state is accessible via a robust GraphQL endpoint (api.rize.io/api/v1/graphql). This enables precise, strongly-typed queries and mutations for custom internal integrations. Concurrently, a Webhook engine allows developers to register receiver URLs. The Rize backend emits real-time HTTP POST payloads whenever core lifecycle events occur (specifically session.created, task.updated, or time_entry.created), facilitating event-driven architectures.Visual Mapping & UI Elements: The platform provides a native GraphQL Playground (/api/v1/graphiql) for in-browser schema exploration and query testing. Webhooks are configured in Settings > Integrations > Webhooks.Data Displayed: The GraphQL Playground displays an interactive schema explorer (Documentation Explorer), raw JSON request bodies, and syntax-highlighted response payloads.User Flow & Micro-interactions: A developer inputs a query string into the Playground. The editor features auto-completion against the live schema. Hitting the "Play" button executes the query, rendering the JSON response in the adjacent pane. To configure a webhook, the user pastes their endpoint URL into the Webhook settings modal and selects the event triggers from a checklist array.Navigation ArchitectureThe spatial hierarchy of Rize prioritizes a left-to-right flow of information density, typical of advanced macOS productivity applications. The architecture relies heavily on persistent sidebars and transient overlays to minimize context switching and cognitive load.Global Sidebar (Left): The primary anchor for navigation. It houses the universal power toggle for the tracking engine at the absolute bottom. The core routing menu contains distinct tabs for core workflows: Home (Daily review and timelines), Productivity (Trends, Goals, Insights), Workspaces (Clients, Projects, Tasks), and Integrations. A persistent, global hotkey (H) instantly routes the view back to the Home tab from anywhere in the application.Top Navigation Tabs: Contextual sub-routing within a primary view. When on the Home tab, the top bar provides horizontal navigation between Activity, Time Entries, Sessions, and Calendar views. The far right of the top bar houses global controls for temporal scope (Day/Week/Month/Year toggles), which filter the data presented in the central viewport.Central Viewport: The primary interaction stage. Depending on the selected sidebar and top-tab routes, this dynamic area renders the vertical timeline, data tables (for rules or workspaces), or dense analytical charts.Contextual Drawers (Right): Transient, sliding panels invoked by interaction within the central viewport. These drawers (such as the Suggestion Review Panel or the Daily Summary panel) slide in from the right edge, providing deep, editable context without forcing a full-page reload or losing the user's spatial anchor on the main timeline.Settings Overlay: Invoked via the global hotkey (.), this view overtakes the central viewport to provide categorized administrative and user preferences, broken down into detailed sub-menus (Privacy, Tracking Rules, Time Entries, Distraction Blocker). Utilizing a hotkey for settings ensures power users can rapidly adjust rules without clicking through nested menus.
